fund-js/05-objects-and-prototypes/01-saf.solution:
const FORBIDDEN = new Set(['__proto__', 'constructor', 'prototype']);
const isPlain = (v) => typeof v === 'object' && v !== null && !Array.isArray(v);

export function deepMerge(base, override) {
  const out = {};
  for (const src of [base, override]) {
    for (const key of Object.keys(src)) {
      if (FORBIDDEN.has(key)) continue;
      const value = src[key];
      out[key] = isPlain(out[key]) && isPlain(value) ? deepMerge(out[key], value) : isPlain(value) ? deepMerge({}, value) : value;
    }
  }
  return out;
}

fund-js/05-objects-and-prototypes/01-saf.explanation:
Copy own keys only, skip the three dangerous names, and recurse only when both sides are plain objects. Recursing into `{}` for an override-only object also clones it, so the result never shares nested objects with the inputs:

```js
for (const src of [base, override]) {
  for (const key of Object.keys(src)) {
    if (FORBIDDEN.has(key)) continue;
    const value = src[key];
    out[key] = isPlain(out[key]) && isPlain(value) ? deepMerge(out[key], value)
             : isPlain(value) ? deepMerge({}, value) : value;
  }
}
```

`Object.keys` returns the parsed own `"__proto__"` key, which is why the explicit skip is needed: `out['__proto__'] = ...` would set the result's prototype.
